WORKLOAD FEATURE — API AUDIT (A to Z)
=======================================
Audited: 2026-08-19 | Scope: src/pages/Workload/WorkloadPage.jsx + supporting components
Implemented: 2026-08-19 — see section 4 for the logic; live-tested against
real backend data (resource_list + get_projectList), verified correct
(9h shift × 100% = 9h/day, 9h × 25% = 2.25h/day, etc.)

STATUS: LIVE — no longer dummy data. Computed client-side from
resource_list + get_projectList (no backend endpoint needed/requested).
New file: src/utils/workloadCalc.js (aggregateWorkload + helpers).
WorkloadPage.jsx now fetches RESOURCE_LIST + GET_PROJECT_LIST + HOLIDAYS
directly and calls aggregateWorkload() instead of GET_WORKLOAD.
Dummy data (workloadDummyData.js) is now ONLY the true-failure fallback
(network/API error), not the default path.


1. CURRENT STATE
-----------------
- Page: src/pages/Workload/WorkloadPage.jsx
- On mount, calls API_ENDPOINTS.GET_WORKLOAD (POST /api/admin/get_workload)
- LIVE-VERIFIED (curl, 2026-08-19): returns HTTP 404 — route does not exist on backend.
- On failure (always, right now), silently falls back to:
    src/data/workloadDummyData.js  → { activeProject, resources }
  and shows an amber "Demo Preview (Awaiting Backend API)" banner (usingFallback flag).
- Everything downstream (KPI cards, table, heatmap cells, search, role filter,
  pagination, monthly/daily view toggle, hours/percentage unit toggle) already
  works correctly against whatever shape it's given — no frontend changes
  needed once real data arrives, PROVIDED the shape matches (see section 2).


2. EXACT DATA SHAPE THE FRONTEND EXPECTS
------------------------------------------
Response contract (data.data must contain both keys):

{
  "success": true,
  "data": {
    "activeProject": {
      "id": string,
      "name": string,
      "priority": "High" | "Medium" | "Low",
      "methodology": string,       // e.g. "Predictive", "Agile Scrum"
      "startDate": "YYYY-MM-DD",
      "progress": number           // 0-100
    },
    "resources": [
      {
        "id": string,
        "name": string,
        "role": string,
        "color": string,           // Tailwind classes e.g. "bg-blue-100 text-blue-700"
                                    // (backend doesn't need to send this — frontend
                                    // can derive a color from role/id if omitted;
                                    // flag to backend team as optional)
        "projects": [
          {
            "id": string,
            "name": string,
            "weekdayHours": [number, number, number, number, number], // Mon-Fri hours
            "monthly": [number x 12],   // pre-aggregated hours per calendar month
            "exceptions": { "<monthIndex 0-11>": true }  // e.g. planned leave clash
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}

Notes:
- weekdayHours is a 5-length array (Mon..Fri) — used for the "Daily" view's
  per-weekday jitter/base value and for the overcapacity KPI calc (sum > 40h/week).
- monthly is a 12-length array, ALREADY aggregated per month by backend/whoever
  builds this — frontend does not recompute it from weekdayHours (see
  workloadDummyData.js's buildMonthly() — that's demo-only math, not something
  the real integration should replicate; backend should just send monthly totals).
- exceptions is sparse — only include months that need the "Exception" heatmap
  color (amber), e.g. approved leave, holiday clash.
- capacity constants already exist client-side, no need from backend:
    MONTHLY_CAPACITY = 176 (22 working days x 8h)
    DAILY_CAPACITY = 8


3. BACKEND ENDPOINT — NO LONGER THE PLAN (superseded by section 4)
-----------------------------------------------
API_ENDPOINTS.GET_WORKLOAD (POST /api/admin/get_workload) still exists in
src/config/api.js and still 404s live — but per section 4, this is NOT being
requested from backend anymore. Workload will be computed client-side from
resource_list + get_projectList's resource_allocations instead. Leaving this
entry in api.js as a documented dead-end (matches the file's own comment)
rather than removing it, in case that decision changes later.


4. CONFIRMED LOGIC — NO NEW BACKEND ENDPOINT NEEDED
---------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIRMED by product owner (2026-08-19): Workload does NOT need a dedicated
get_workload endpoint. It's computed on the frontend from two EXISTING, LIVE
data sources — same pattern already used for ResourceAllocationPage (reused
GET_PROJECT_LIST's resource_allocations field instead of requesting a new API):

  a) GET /api/admin/resource_list (GET method, live, verified)
     Per resource: name, role, start_time, end_time, shift, and
     monday..sunday boolean flags (which days that resource works).
     → This is the resource's SHIFT / daily capacity, e.g. start_time
       "09:00:00", end_time "17:00:00" = 8 working hours/day.

  b) POST /api/admin/get_projectList → resource_allocations (JSON string field,
     already used by ResourceAllocationPage.jsx)
     Per project, per allocated resource: role, resourceName, allocation
     (PERCENTAGE, not hours), workingDays (array of day names).
     → This is how much of that daily capacity is committed to THIS project.

THE CALCULATION (confirmed):
  dailyCapacityHours = end_time − start_time   (from resource_list, per resource)
  dailyAllocatedHours = dailyCapacityHours × (allocation% / 100)   (per project)

  Worked example: shift 09:00–17:00 = 8h capacity. Allocation = 25%.
  → dailyAllocatedHours = 8 × 0.25 = 2 hours/day on that project.

  This applies to each day in that resource's workingDays for that project —
  days NOT in workingDays get 0 (shows as "Non Working" / null in the heatmap,
  same as weekends already do in workloadDummyData.js's dailyValueFor()).

  weekdayHours[Mon..Fri] = dailyAllocatedHours if that weekday is in
  workingDays, else 0 — this directly matches the shape frontend already
  expects (see section 2), so ResourceRow/ProjectRow/WorkLoadTable need NO
  changes once this is wired.

  monthly[i] = sum of dailyAllocatedHours over however many of that
  resource's working days fall in calendar month i (NOT the flat
  weekTotal × 4.33 × MONTH_MULTIPLIER demo math in workloadDummyData.js —
  that was only ever a placeholder to fake seasonal-looking variance; the
  real version should count actual working days per month, which itself
  needs a real calendar — reuse the existing HOLIDAYS endpoint + the
  Standard/Custom working-day logic already built for the Gantt chart's
  calendar feature, so weekends/holidays are excluded consistently across
  the whole app instead of a second, separate calendar concept).

MULTI-PROJECT RESOURCE:
  A resource can appear in resource_allocations on MULTIPLE projects
  simultaneously — sum each project's dailyAllocatedHours separately per
  resource (matches resources[].projects[] shape already, one entry per
  project) rather than collapsing into one number. The existing
  "Overcapacity" KPI (sum > 40h/week across ALL of a resource's projects)
  already assumes this multi-project structure — confirms this is the
  right shape to build toward.

  Edge case to watch: if a resource's allocations across all their projects
  sum to over 100% of daily capacity (e.g. 70% + 60% = 130%), that IS a
  real overallocation and should show as such (rose/"overallocated" status),
  not be capped/normalized down — this is exactly the scenario the
  Overcapacity Risk KPI card exists to catch.

AGGREGATION WORK NEEDED (frontend, not backend):
  resource_allocations is currently read ONE PROJECT AT A TIME
  (ResourceAllocationPage fetches get_projectList and flattens allocations
  per-project-row). Workload needs the INVERSE grouping: fetch get_projectList
  (all projects), parse resource_allocations on each, then group by
  resourceName/resource_id ACROSS all projects so one resource's card shows
  every project they're staffed on. This cross-project grouping utility does
  not exist yet — net-new frontend work, not present in ResourceAllocationPage
  or anywhere else today.

RESOURCE IDENTITY CAVEAT:
  resource_allocations rows join resources by resourceName (string) in some
  cases and resource_id/tl_id in others (seen inconsistently in
  ResourceAllocationPage's flattenAllocations() — id: row.id ?? row.resource_id
  ?? row.tl_id ?? "-"). Matching a resource_allocations row back to its
  resource_list entry (to get start_time/end_time) needs a reliable key —
  verify with live data whether resource_id is always populated and matches
  resource_list.id, or whether name-matching is the only reliable join in
  practice (name-matching is fragile if two resources share a name).


5. OPEN QUESTIONS (mostly answered now — remaining ones only)
--------------------------------------
[x] Does "hours" mean planned/allocated hours? ANSWERED — yes, derived from
    shift capacity × allocation%, not timesheet/logged hours. No timesheet
    table needed.
[ ] Scope: org-wide (all PMs' resources) or per-logged-in-PM? Current dummy
    data + UI (KPI cards, table) assume org-wide — confirm this matches
    intended PMO-level visibility, not filtered to one PM's team. Since
    get_projectList already returns all projects (not PM-scoped), org-wide
    is the natural default unless told otherwise.
[ ] Which calendar defines "working day" for the monthly aggregation —
    the resource's own workingDays (from resource_allocations, per project)
    only, or should org holidays (HOLIDAYS endpoint) also zero out a day
    even if it's in workingDays? Recommend: yes, subtract holidays too, for
    consistency with how the Gantt "Standard (Mon-Fri + Holidays)" calendar
    already works elsewhere in this app.
[ ] activeProject header: with real data, a resource can be on N projects —
    does the single "activeProject" banner at the top of the page still make
    sense, or should it become "the project currently being filtered/viewed"
    (i.e. a filter driving the table), or be removed in favor of just the
    per-resource multi-project breakdown the table already shows? This is a
    product/UX call, not a data-availability one — flag for whoever owns
    the Workload page design.


6. SUMMARY
-----------
Frontend: DONE, production-ready, waiting on the aggregation logic in
section 4 to be implemented (client-side, no backend dependency).
Backend:  NOTHING needed — resource_list + get_projectList already have
what's required. get_workload endpoint is no longer being requested.
Remaining work is a frontend-only aggregation utility (group
resource_allocations by resource across all projects, compute hours from
each resource's own shift capacity — see section 4 for the exact formula
and open edge cases).
